VERSION 1.6.0
-------------

SIGNIFICANT USER-VISIBLE CHANGES

    o Small change in get_h5mread_returned_type() / h5mread().
      get_h5mread_returned_type() now returns "double" instead of "integer"
      if the H5 type of the dataset to read is a 32-bit **unsigned** integer
      type (e.g. H5T_NATIVE_UINT32).
      This means that h5mread() now returns an array of type() "double"
      instead of "integer" when reading such dataset. This avoids truncation
      of values that are >= 2^31.
      Note that the user can always force the returned type() to "integer" by
      calling h5mread() with 'as.integer=TRUE' if they know that the dataset
      does not contain values >= 2^31 or if they don't care that big values
      get truncated.

BUG FIXES

    o Fix h5mread() on H5T_VARIABLE dataset with NULL pointers.
      Starting with rhdf5 2.57.10, NULL pointers in an H5T_VARIABLE dataset
      are interpreted as NAs. So h5mread() now:
      - works on an H5T_VARIABLE dataset with NULL pointers (used to crash!);
      - interprets NULL pointers as NAs like rhdf5::h5read() does.


